JSON.stringify converts a JavaScript value into a JSON string. It omits functions, undefined, and symbols, and it throws on circular references or BigInt values.

code:
const s = JSON.stringify({ name: 'Ana', fn: () => {} });
console.log(s);  // {"name":"Ana"}

real example:
Sending API fixtures logs the wrapped object, and knowing functions are dropped explains surprising payloads in captured requests.

JSON.stringify throws TypeError: Converting circular structure to JSON. You must break the cycle or replace the deeply nested fields before serializing.

code:
const obj = { a: {} };
obj.a.self = obj;
// JSON.stringify(obj);  // throws

real example:
Playwright page objects sometimes hold references to the browser context; only send plain data objects when logging or posting them.

The replacer is a function or array that filters or transforms values during serialization, which can hide sensitive fields.

code:
const data = { user: 'Ana', token: 'secret' };
const safe = JSON.stringify(data, (key, value) =>
  key === 'token' ? undefined : value
);
console.log(safe);  // {"user":"Ana"}

real example:
Logging request payloads while stripping passwords or tokens avoids leaking secrets into CI logs.

The reviver function transforms each parsed key-value pair, often converting date strings back into Date objects after parsing.

code:
const text = '{"created": "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"}';
const obj = JSON.parse(text, (k, v) =>
  k === 'created' ? new Date(v) : v
);
console.log(obj.created instanceof Date);  // true

real example:
Reviving timestamps lets tests assert date ordering directly instead of comparing ISO strings.

fetch(url) defaults to a GET request and returns a promise that resolves to a Response object. Call res.json() to read the body.

code:
const res = await fetch('https://api.test.com/users');
const users = await res.json();
console.log(users[0].name);

real example:
API smoke tests use fetch against a real backend, with assertions on res.status and the parsed body.

Pass an options object with method, headers, and a body stringified with JSON.stringify so the server receives proper JSON content.

code:
const res = await fetch('https://api.test.com/login', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
  body: JSON.stringify({ user: 'admin', pass: 'secret' }),
});

real example:
Auth setup before UI steps posts a login request to create an authenticated session the browser then reuses.

The fetch body must be a string, blob, form data, or similar. Passing an object directly causes a TypeError, so objects become JSON text first.

code:
// fetch(url, { body: {a:1} })  // TypeError
fetch(url, { body: JSON.stringify({ a: 1 }) });

real example:
A common first bug in API tests is forgetting stringify, which crashes immediately with an unhelpful type error.

The Response object exposes res.status and res.ok, where ok is true for status 200-299. fetch does not reject on 4xx or 5xx.

code:
const res = await fetch(url);
console.log(res.status, res.ok);
if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);

real example:
Assertions like expect(res.status).toBe(200) work because fetch resolved even though a 404 is an error response.

res.json() parses the body as JSON and returns the resulting value, while res.text() returns the raw body as a string. Both return promises and consume the response body.

code:
const data = await res.json();
const raw = await res.text();

real example:
When a server returns an HTML error page, res.json() throws but res.text() lets you assert on the message inside it.

fetch rejects with a TypeError only on network-level failures such as DNS errors or connection refused. HTTP errors still resolve, so you must check status yourself.

code:
try {
  const res = await fetch('http://localhost:9999');
} catch (err) {
  console.log('network failure', err);
}

real example:
Wrapping API calls in try/catch turns connection refused into a readable test failure instead of a generic crash.

Use an AbortController with AbortSignal.timeout, which rejects the request after the configured milliseconds.

code:
const res = await fetch(url, {
  signal: AbortSignal.timeout(5000),
});

real example:
Hanging endpoint calls in CI stall entire suites; a 10-second abort turns the delay into a quick, actionable failure.

res.headers is a Headers object; use get or has to read specific header values.

code:
const res = await fetch(url);
console.log(res.headers.get('content-type'));

real example:
Asserting security headers like cache-control or content-type in API tests is done via the headers object.

Collect fetch promises in an array and await Promise.all, which runs them concurrently and returns results in order.

code:
const results = await Promise.all([
  fetch('/a'),
  fetch('/b'),
]);
const data = await Promise.all(results.map(r => r.json()));

real example:
Checking health of several endpoints before a suite starts finishes in the time of the slowest call instead of the sum.

Playwright request contexts reuse the browser's cookies and add retries, and the playwright library request fixture defaults to the baseURL automatically.

code:
const res = await request.get('/api/orders', {
  headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` },
});
const body = await res.json();

real example:
UI tests that also call the API keep cookies in sync by using the built-in Playwright request fixture instead of raw fetch.
